<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1139–<a href="/years/1203">1203</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad of Ghor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad was sultan of the Ghurid dynasty from 1163 to 1203, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozkoh">Firozkoh</a> in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. He shared power with his brother Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad, who led the conquests in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while he himself fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a> is attributed to his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghiyath_al-Din_Muhammad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ghiyath was the son of Baha al-Din Sam I, who reigned briefly over the Ghurids in 1149. According to the Tabaqat-i-Nasiri of Minhaj al-Siraj his birth name, like his younger brother's, was Muhammad; his mother called him Habshi for his dark complexion, and as a prince he bore the title Shamsuddin. He and his brother were imprisoned by their uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a> and released by his son Sayf al-Din Muhammad. When Sayf died in 1163, the Ghurid nobles raised Ghiyath to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With his brother's help Ghiyath killed a rival chief, Abu'l Abbas, and defeated his uncle Fakhr al-Din Masud and the Seljuq governor Yildiz at Ragh-i Zar, then conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindawar">Zamindawar</a>, Badghis, Gharchistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guzgan">Guzgan</a>. He spared his uncle and restored him at Bamiyan. In 1173 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> and installed Mu'izz al-Din there, and two years later he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and Pushang. The rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and the Oghuz of Kirman acknowledged his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><p>In 1186 the brothers captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and ended the Ghaznavid dynasty, executing its last ruler, Khusrau-Malik. In 1190 Ghiyath defeated the Khwarazmian prince Sultan Shah at Marw al-Rudh and annexed most of his lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, then beat off Tekish at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and the Kara-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guzgan">Guzgan</a>. After Tekish died in 1200 the brothers took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, but Mu'izz al-Din's undisciplined advance towards Ray led to their only reported quarrel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ghiyath died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> on 13 March 1203. Mu'izz al-Din hurried back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, won the support of the nobles and was crowned sultan at Firuzkuh. Under the two brothers the Ghurids became one of the great powers of the eastern Islamic world. Ghiyath was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friday-mosque-of-herat">Great Mosque of Herat</a>, in a mausoleum at its northern gate, which was destroyed when the mosque was renovated in the 1950s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many highlanders of Ghor had followed the Karramiyya, a pietist Sunni movement, but <b>Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</b> later adopted the Shafi'i school while Mu'izz al-Din became a Hanafi, and the theologian Fakhr al-Din al-Razi was active at Ghurid courts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.52,65.26&amp;year=1180" aria-label="Ghor Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5370 4605h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5370 4605h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.52,65.26&amp;year=1180">What was here in 1180? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
